17
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
He said: I've made several attempts to read Baden Powell's AIDS to Scout Master's ship,

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
but I don't think I ever made it all the way through without losing the thread.

19
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
It's not a long book, but the 1920s language and ideas required a continual translation in my head.

20
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Clark has set out to write the same kind of advice for today's Scouters with great success.

21
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
This is easy to read and a great introduction to what it means to be an adult leader in Scouting-

22
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Highly recommended.

23
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Thanks so much, Walter.

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
I really do appreciate that.

25
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Once again, the book he's talking about is called The Scouting Journey

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,000
and it was my goal in that book to take all of the ideas from Baden Powell's AIDS to Scout Master's ship,

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
which is couched in language that a lot of people, including myself and apparently Walter,

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
find. you know. you've got to kind of wade through it.

29
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
It's a little bit of a slog

30
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
And what I wanted to do was to take those ideas and update them and make them more readable and accessible for people today,

31
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And I think I was reasonably successful in doing that.

32
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Check out The Scouting Journey.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,000
It's on Amazon.

34
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
I'll have a link in the podcast notes.

35
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Also heard from John Dyer about last week's podcast number 313..

36
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
He said an excellent review of the new guide.

37
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
I enjoyed listening to the two of you discussing the content.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Thank you, John, for that.

39
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
When I posted last week's podcast on Facebook there was a little discussion that started with somebody saying:

40
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
you know they never should have changed the name of this from the Scout Master Handbook

41
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
And it was a pretty weak excuse

42
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
And my opinion is just watering down scouting,

43
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
And you know I really take exception to that.

44
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
I don't think this is watering anything down.

45
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:14,000
I think it was expanding the idea that the information in this book is for all scouters involved with a scout troop

46
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
And it's very helpful for them to have it and to be able to reference it.

47
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
So calling it something other than the Scout Master Handbook was probably a very good idea

48
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
And a couple of people agreed with me on that.

49
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
And then, to be really argumentative, someone mentioned that it shouldn't really be called the troop leader handbook

50
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
because the only real leaders in a troop are the scouts,

51
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
And I agree in principle. but nobody asked me what this publication should be titled.

52
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
If they had asked, I would have agreed and said let's call it the troop scout or guidebook or something like that.

53
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
And you know it does do a very good job of explaining

54
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Adults. are there not so much as directive, leaders, guides and mentors to the scouts?

55
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
I'm so happy with the content of the book,

56
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
I don't want a nitpick with the title,

57
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Okay. so

