who both became patrons since last week's podcast.

80
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
In this week's podcast we're going to catch up on email questions that have come in over the past several weeks

81
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
And I'm going to limit myself to answering four of them. otherwise we would be here for a while.

82
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:09,000
If you haven't heard your question answered on the podcast, try and answer them as soon as I possibly can by email.

83
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
But if you haven't heard yours on the podcast, that's because there's a bit of a backlog

84
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:20,000
And we're going to take care of four of those questions on this podcast. so let's get started, shall we?

85
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

86
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

87
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

88
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
You were always on the beat Boy, beat Boy.

89
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Yeah, you were always on the beat Boy, beat Boy.

90
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
I'm hanging in the street Boy, street Boy.

91
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
We were dancing to the beat Boy, beat Boy.

92
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Yeah, you're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

93
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

94
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

95
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Write me a letter, Send it by name.

96
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
Couple of the emails that we'll be talking about this week will remain anonymous

97
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
for reasons that will readily become apparent as soon as we delve into them,

98
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and I'm perfectly happy to not mention anything about where emails have come from.

99
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,000
but I think it's particularly important, especially when we're dealing with sensitive or difficult questions,

100
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
to just be able to share the question without any personal information being shared,

101
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
because I think it helps everybody out.

102
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:59,000
So I had this email that begins. while I certainly enjoy the informative and entertaining nature of the Scout Master Podcast,

103
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:06,000
What keeps me coming back is your experience and ability to see through seemingly difficult situations with care and guidance.

104
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
One of our adult volunteers passed away suddenly,

105
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
and there really isn't a playbook for this.

106
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,000
What role do I have as a scouter?

107
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
What role does our troop have?

108
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
How can we help the Scout?

109
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
How can we help the family?

110
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Well, as I read your email, I was reminded of an experience about a decade ago

111
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
when the father of two of my Scouts, two brothers, took his own life.

112
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:39,000
It was sudden, it was horrifying, and I had no idea what to do next.

113
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
I mean, what do I say to these two boys.

114
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
What do I say to the other Scouts?

115
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
I realized I was way out of my depth

116
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and thankfully I had a connection to a professional counselor who I contacted

117
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and she and a colleague offered to be at our next troop meeting

118
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
to talk with the Scouts about what had happened.

119
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
This counselor done a lot of work with youth

120
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
and I felt very comfortable in taking her up on that offer.

121
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
Now I had no idea of the kind of a meeting this would be,

122
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
how things would go, what would be said or how it would all end up,

123
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
and I guess I could credit myself for knowing I was out of my depth

124
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:25,000
and asking for help. but to be honest, I was horrified at the prospect

125
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
that I would do further damage if I took it on by myself.

126
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
The boys who lost their dad came to that meeting along with their uncle.

127
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
The counselors divided us into two groups and spent the rest of the time

128
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
facilitating a discussion with the Scouts.

129
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
I was not sure I had done the right thing at all

130
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
and I was hardly able to listen to the conversations, much less participate in them.

131
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,000
but when we dismissed the boys and uncle told me it was tremendously helpful

132
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
and it was a great relief to him personally

133
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:57,000
and we went on from there and I don't really recall any issues coming up after that session,

134
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
and I could write a lot about what I learned,

135
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
but the single takeaway from the experience was recognizing

136
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
that I was really out of my depth and asking for help,

137
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
and that's my best advice to you

138
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
or anyone who finds themselves in a similar circumstance:

139
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
get some professional help with this, if at all possible,

140
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
from people who are trained and experienced with helping children

141
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
process these kind of losses,

142
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
because, as you might imagine, the loss of a parent

143
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,000
is not simply upsetting to the children in that family,

144
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,000
but any children who those children may be associated with.

145
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,000
I know that it may be a little difficult for some of us to go.

146
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
you know I'm really way out of my depth here.

147
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
I really need to have help. I can't handle this,

148
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
but these are very serious issues

149
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
and I would liken it to this:

150
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
if one of your scouts was seriously injured,

151
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
you would know when you are out of your depth

152
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
as far as what you're able to do for that injury. right,

153
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
you're going to call the EMTs, you're going to get them to an emergency room,

154
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and this is the same sort of a situation

155
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
you're out of your depth, you don't really know what to do next.

156
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:07,000
don't ever be afraid to ask for help in a situation like this.

157
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
I was fortunate that within the circle of my friends,

158
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
I knew somebody who was a professional counselor and could help out.

159
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
if I hadn't, I would have widened that circle

160
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
until I found somebody who was able to speak with me about it.

161
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
so that is what my advice would be:

162
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
when you're handling any kind of an issue

163
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
where you feel, as a scouter, that you're out of your depth,

164
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:27,000
don't be afraid to ask for help.

165
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
our second question for this podcast also remains anonymous,

166
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and it begins this way:

167
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
I found out a couple days after a recent camp out

168
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
one of my older scouts was teaching younger scouts

169
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
some very profane, explicit limericks,

170
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:45,000
and I am really pretty upset about the whole thing.

171
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
I understand that we're not supposed to punish scouts,

172
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
but I really question the judgment of this older first class scout

173
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
who's ready to advance to STAR,

174
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
and I am suggesting that we delay his rank advancement for four months.

175
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
what advice do you have for me concerning the advancement issues specifically-

176
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and I'm open to any other advice you might have about this-

177
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
well, I wish I could say that this never happened to me.

178
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
this sort of an issue is going to happen

179
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
when you are working with boys this age.

180
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
boys this age do this kind of thing.

181
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
this is not to have a. boys will be boys,

182
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
dismissing the behavior attitude towards it.

183
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
but we need to understand why they do this sort of thing.

184
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:27,000
this isn't some kind of basic moral failure on their part.

185
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
it made perfect sense for him to do this. okay,

186
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
I know that if I were this that age

187
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and I knew some off-color jokes

188
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
or dirty limericks or something like that,

189
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
I would think that they were the best thing ever

190
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and I would make sure to share them with my friends,

191
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
and especially with younger people,

192
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
because I would make this equation. they would think I was pretty cool.

193
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
so it's a win-win situation as far as I can see,

194
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
of course, unless somebody finds out that I was doing it.

195
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
but I'm not really worried about that at that age.

196
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
I mean, of course, this scout knows better.

197
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
he probably feels pretty bad about the whole thing

198
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
or at least embarrassed that somebody caught him,

199
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
because teenagers are very poor estimators of risk.

200
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
they tend to value the positive outcomes

201
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
of taking a risky action like this

202
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
way way above the negative outcomes.

203
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
that's why we have something like a juvenile justice system

204
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
and emergency rooms. okay,

205
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
teenagers make very poor decisions

206
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
when it comes to things like this,

207
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
and by doing that, this is a process

208
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
by which they learn to make better decisions.

209
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
just about every young person that we know,

210
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
including the ones that you're a parent to,

211
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,000
that live in your house,

212
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
no matter how great and wonderful a kid they are,

213
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,000
they sometimes just do a stupid, stupid thing.

214
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,000
that is just so jaw-droppingly stupid

215
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
it stops you in their tracks.

216
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
theoretically, of course, they know better,

217
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
but they don't experientially know better

218
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
until they push the limits a little bit,

219
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
and I think that's what's going on here.

220
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
I know it's what I would have done at that age.

221
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
so what do you do next?

222
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,000
well, if you come at this from the punishment direction,

223
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
this scout is going to do what we all did at that age.

224
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,000
he'll do a passable job of saying that he's sorry

225
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
and act repentant in front of adults,

226
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and then he will likely kind of thumb his nose

227
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,000
behind your back,

228
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:28,000
because he knows that adults are somewhat hypocritical

229
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:29,000
in this area

230
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
and that adults know plenty of raunchy jokes

231
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and swear once in a while,

232
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,000
and so there is an inherent unfairness that's going on here,

233
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
coming at them with a punishment

234
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
is going to backfire on you.

235
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
plus, we don't punish scouts.

236
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
we just don't do that,

237
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
and I've talked about this many, many times before.

238
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
punishment never happens in scouting,

239
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,000
should never, ever happen.

240
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
proportionate consequences happen.

241
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:56,000
if you make a mess,

242
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,000
you clean up the mess.

243
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:58,000
if you break something,

244
00:14:58,000 --> 00:14:59,000
you fix something.

245
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
if you share an off-collar joke

246
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
with a bunch of younger scouts,

247
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
you apologize for having done so,

248
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:06,000
but you don't get punished

249
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
for any of those circumstances.

250
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
you just deal with the proportionate consequences.

251
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
anything above those proportionate consequences,

252
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,000
that's punishment.

253
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Scouters don't punish.

254
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
parents can have the option to punish

255
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
if they think it's going to be useful for them

256
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and that's the way they want to handle it.

257
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
so we make sure to let parents know what's happened

258
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
and it's their choice to punish

259
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:28,000
or not to punish.

260
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
the other thing that is really important here

261
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,000
is the kind of conversation that you can have with this scout.

262
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
if you just like, look at the situation

263
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
and you issue a judgment

264
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
and that judgment is followed up with some kind of punishment,

265
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
like making him wait an extra four months

266
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:44,000
before he can be a star scout.

267
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
you've missed a really big opportunity.

268
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
that four months is going to go by.

269
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
he's going to forget about that.

270
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,000
but if you have a really meaningful, open conversation

271
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:55,000
about the scout oath and law,

272
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
he'll probably remember that conversation

273
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
longer than he will remember that punishment.

274
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
so sit down with the scout

275
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:02,000
and start asking some questions.

276
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
why would anybody be upset

277
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,000
about what he did?

278
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
why have him explain that to you?

279
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
does he think that the scout

280
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
oath and law mean anything,

281
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
or are they just empty words that we repeat?

282
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
what would he do in response

283
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:16,000
to a situation like this

284
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
if he were in your shoes?

285
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
does he think what he did was wrong?

286
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
can he tell you why it was wrong?

287
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
does his conscience tell him

288
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
that he was acting in the best interest

289
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:27,000
of those younger scouts,

290
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
that he was demonstrating the kind of scout spirit

291
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
we'd expect of a star scout?

292
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
I mean, this is a very meaningful discussion here.

293
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
when you have the chance to get this scout

294
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
to exercise their own internal standard,

295
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
to explore the ideas behind the words

296
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,000
of the scout oath and law,

297
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
you won't be able to have that conversation

298
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
if you decide to pronounce judgment

299
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:46,000
and issue a punishment

300
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,000
as a part of the conversation.

301
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:48,000
you have to make it clear:

302
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
if this kind of thing becomes a pattern of behavior,

303
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
well, he won't be able to continue in scouts.

304
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:53,000
it's pretty simple,

305
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and that you need to have a discussion

306
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
with his parents about the whole issue,

307
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
because it's your responsibility to his parents

308
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
to share this sort of thing with them

309
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,000
and they may choose to punish him

310
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,000
kind of out of your hands,

311
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
but that he needs to understand

312
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
why you have to talk to his parents about it

313
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,000
and you want them there

314
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:09,000
while you talk to the parents.

315
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
this will be as big an issue

316
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
as you decide to make out of it,

317
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:14,000
and I would make it

318
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
as small an issue as possible.

319
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:17,000
scouts are smart.

320
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
they understand when they've done things wrong.

321
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
we don't have to bludgeon them over the head about it

322
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:22,000
for days and days

323
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,000
or for four months.

324
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
let's talk about it honestly and openly,

325
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
use it as an opportunity

326
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
to leverage their growing,

327
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
their understanding of the scout oath and law,

328
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
make sure that any punishment

329
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
responsibilities are right where they belong-

330
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
with his parents, at home-

331
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:38,000
and move on

332
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
and put a positive spin on what is

333
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:42,000
honestly, yes,

334
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
very frustrating for a scouter

335
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,000
to have to deal with things like this,

336
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,000
but if we take it as an opportunity

337
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
to help a scout

338
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
form and shape their character

339
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
through the application of the scout oath and law,

340
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
it becomes a very positive thing.

341
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
so our third email this week

342
00:17:58,000 --> 00:17:59,000
comes from Christopher Taylor.

343
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:00,000
he says: I'm a long time fan

344
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
and assistant scout master for a few years,

345
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
and I was looking for guidelines

346
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
on making a Facebook group

347
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,000
for our scout troop.

348
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
if I did create a Facebook group,

349
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,000
it would be private, of course,

350
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
with invite request admission only.

351
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
your thoughts would be appreciated.

352
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:17,000
well, Christopher,

353
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:18,000
I sent you to a link

354
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
from the Boy Scouts of America

355
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and their official position

356
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:23,000
on the use of social media

357
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
and it will also be in the podcast notes

358
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
and you'll note on that page.

359
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,000
it says this:

360
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
to help ensure that all communications

361
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
on social media channels remain positive

362
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:33,000
and safe,

363
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
these channels must be public.

364
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
therefore, no private channels,

365
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
eg private Facebook groups

366
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
or invite only YouTube channels etc.

367
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
are acceptable in helping administer

368
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:46,000
the scouting program.

369
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:49,000
so that's the BSA position on the matter.

370
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
my personal advice to you,

371
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,000
after having tried out a number of these things,

372
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
is to just leave them alone

373
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
and don't get involved with

374
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
trying to set up a troop page

375
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:01,000
for your Facebook

376
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,000
or anything like that,

377
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
because they're not a very good way

378
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:05,000
to communicate with your Scouts

379
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:06,000
or their families.

380
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
I know it seems like a good idea,

381
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
but it's likely not to be,

382
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
because there's really no closed loop

383
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
communication involved

384
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
where a person at the other end of the communication

385
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
acknowledges that they've received it.

386
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and when you set up a group like this,

387
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,000
whether it's private or public

388
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:22,000
or whatever,

389
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
it can take constant monitoring

390
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and there are potential problems

391
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:27,000
with youth members and adults

392
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
friending each other on Facebook

393
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,000
and not being able to monitor

394
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
the resulting interactions

395
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
and the inevitable misunderstandings

396
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,000
that can arise from these kind of interactions

397
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:38,000
between youth members,

398
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:39,000
between adult members.

399
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
you get where I'm saying

400
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:42,000
now. I wrote about this

401
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
about three or four years ago.

402
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
I'll have a link to those articles

403
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
in the podcast notes.

404
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
but to give you the quick analysis,

405
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
while all of these social media tools

406
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and things like that

407
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
do have their uses

408
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
and they are useful for some things in Scouting,

409
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
in my experience-

410
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and other people may be able to do this successfully-

411
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
but in my experience

412
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
they don't work for unit communications

413
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
and they're really a pretty bad idea

414
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:11,000
because of the other potentials

415
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
that I just mentioned.

416
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,000
I go back far enough to remember pre-email days

417
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
and we tried lots of different things.

418
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
we tried paper newsletters

419
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
mailed out going home.

420
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
those helped a little bit.

421
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
we tried having a recorded phone message

422
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:27,000
where you could call up

423
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and get a phone message about what was happening.

424
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
and then, of course, email came along

425
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
and we all thought, hallelujah,

426
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
all our communication problems are fixed.

427
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
that turned out to be the case, did it?

428
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
then Facebook and the other

429
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
social media things came along.

430
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
we thought, finally, we'll have something to fix this.

431
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
then things like Scoutbook come along

432
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,000
and everything. we think

433
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:51,000
now we don't have to worry about communications anymore.

434
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
well, none of these things are 100% solutions.

435
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:55,000
some of them may help,

436
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
but when you add up

437
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
the amount of time that you have to

438
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
maintain and monitor them,

439
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:04,000
here's what I believe you're going to end up finding out.

440
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
the world standard is one person

441
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
talking to another person,

442
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,000
that's it.

443
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
whether that's over a telephone

444
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:12,000
or face to face,

445
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:13,000
it really doesn't matter.

446
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
but the only way you actually

447
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:18,000
have a reliable communication with somebody

448
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:21,000
that I like to call a closed loop communication,

449
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,000
where they say: yes,

450
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:23,000
I have heard what you said

451
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:24,000
and I understand it,

452
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
is through that face to face

453
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
or voice to voice contact.

454
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:29,000
my advice:

455
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:30,000
if you really want to

456
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
up your communication with everybody

457
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
to make sure people are understanding things,

458
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:35,000
I would put all that energy

459
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:38,000
into making sure that everybody gets a phone call,

460
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and even that is not 100%.

461
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
human beings is our greatest asset

462
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and our biggest problem.

463
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,000
communication is always a challenge,

464
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:48,000
no matter where you are

465
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:49,000
or what you're doing.

466
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
if you're getting more than three people together

467
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:52,000
around a given goal

468
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
or something like that.

469
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:57,000
communication is always going to be a challenge,

470
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:58,000
and I am not suggesting

471
00:21:58,000 --> 00:21:59,000
people haven't figured out a way

472
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:00,000
to use social media

473
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
more effectively than maybe I did,

474
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
but I still think everybody would have to agree

475
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
that there's only one gold standard:

476
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:07,000
face to face,

477
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:08,000
voice to voice-

478
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and that's where I think

479
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
your effort is better spent.

480
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:15,000
and our fourth and final email

481
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
will be anonymous.

482
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
here's what it says.

483
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
I have a situation developing

484
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:22,000
over the past few months.

485
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
I'm hoping you can offer me

486
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:25,000
some sound advice.

487
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:26,000
I'm a scout master

488
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
and one of our newer parents

489
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:29,000
has become a problem.

490
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:30,000
they are very critical

491
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
of what my older scouts are doing.

492
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
they are stepping in constantly

493
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:35,000
with comments

494
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
that are basically undoing

495
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:38,000
what I'm trying to accomplish

496
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:39,000
as a scout master.

497
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
I'm looking for a second opinion

498
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:42,000
and any offer of advice

499
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:43,000
how to resolve this.

500
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
I want us to find a happy place

501
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
where we can all win.

502
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
well, I'm sorry to hear this is going on.

503
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
don't feel alone.

504
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
it happens all the time in scouting

505
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
and outside of scouting too.

506
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:56,000
every once in a while,

507
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
our scouts, our youth members,

508
00:22:58,000 --> 00:22:59,000
may cause conflicts,

509
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
and these are usually

510
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:02,000
pretty easily resolved,

511
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
in comparison

512
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
to the kind of conflicts

513
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
and difficulties that adults create

514
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
distressingly, this is not

515
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
unusual or infrequent.

516
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
now, if you're a scouter,

517
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
and especially if you're a scout master,

518
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
that means you are a good-hearted person,

519
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
and this good-heartedness

520
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
serves you and your scouts

